studio|At 48 kHz, how long is a 480-sample audio buffer?|10 milliseconds|1 millisecond|48 milliseconds|100 milliseconds|480 divided by 48,000 seconds equals 10 ms.|ALAT
studio|If two identical audio signals are summed perfectly in phase, what is the approximate level increase?|6 dB|3 dB|0 dB|12 dB|Doubling identical signal amplitude adds about 6 dB.|DIG
studio|What generally happens to monitoring latency when buffer size drops from 512 to 256 samples?|It decreases|It doubles|It becomes exactly zero|It is unrelated to buffers|Smaller buffers reduce the time each audio chunk spends waiting.|ALAT
studio|What trade-off commonly comes with using a smaller audio buffer?|Higher CPU demand|Lower pitch|Fewer audio channels by definition|Automatic mastering|Lower buffers reduce latency but can increase CPU load and dropouts.|ALAT
studio|In standard balanced XLR phantom powering, which pins receive equal DC voltage?|Pins 2 and 3|Pins 1 and 2|Pins 1 and 3|Only pin 1|Phantom voltage is applied equally to signal pins 2 and 3 relative to pin 1.|SPH
studio|Does a standard balanced moving-coil dynamic microphone need phantom power?|No|Yes, always 48 V|Only above 10 kHz|Only in stereo|Balanced moving-coil dynamics generally work without phantom power.|SPH
studio|Which microphone pattern has its strongest rejection at the sides, around 90 and 270 degrees?|Figure-eight|Omnidirectional|Cardioid|Subcardioid|A figure-eight pattern has side nulls.|SMIC
studio|What is proximity effect on a directional microphone?|Bass boost at close distances|Treble loss caused by cables|Automatic noise gating|Distortion from phantom power|Directional mics often gain low-frequency response as the source moves closer.|SMIC
studio|Which common microphone pattern normally has no proximity effect?|Omnidirectional|Figure-eight|Hypercardioid|Cardioid|Pressure-operated omnidirectional microphones generally lack proximity effect.|SMIC
studio|What can physically damage the thin element in a traditional ribbon microphone?|A strong blast of air|Changing the song key|Low room temperature alone|Using a microphone stand|Ribbon elements can be deformed by strong air blasts.|SMIC
studio|In a condenser microphone, the diaphragm and backplate form what?|A capacitor|A transformer|A speaker coil|A fuse|Their changing capacitance converts sound vibrations into an electrical signal.|SMIC
studio|At 48 kHz, what is the Nyquist frequency?|24 kHz|48 kHz|12 kHz|96 kHz|The Nyquist frequency is half the sample rate.|DIG
studio|What does digital aliasing create when frequencies exceed the Nyquist limit without proper filtering?|False lower-frequency components|More analog headroom|A wider stereo image|Extra bit depth|Undersampled frequencies can fold into the audible band as aliases.|DIG
studio|At 48 kHz, approximately how long is one audio sample?|20.8 microseconds|20.8 milliseconds|2.08 seconds|480 microseconds|One sample lasts 1/48,000 second, about 20.8 microseconds.|DIG
studio|What does plug-in delay compensation primarily keep aligned?|Tracks with different processing delays|Guitar strings at the same tension|Left and right speaker wattage|Microphone phantom voltages|Delay compensation offsets processing latency to keep tracks in time.|ALAT
studio|Why are Bluetooth headphones often discouraged for live software monitoring?|They add significant latency|They always record in mono|They cannot play bass|They require phantom power|Bluetooth audio transmission usually adds substantial monitoring delay.|ALAT
studio|On Windows, which driver protocol is commonly preferred for low-latency audio interfaces?|ASIO|MIDI Clock|DMX512|SMTP|Native ASIO drivers generally offer lower latency than standard Windows audio paths.|ALAT
studio|What is the single-buffer duration of 128 samples at 48 kHz, approximately?|2.67 milliseconds|26.7 milliseconds|0.267 milliseconds|12.8 milliseconds|128 divided by 48,000 seconds is about 2.67 ms.|ALAT
studio|What does 0 dBFS represent in digital PCM audio?|Full-scale digital amplitude|The quietest audible sound|A standard microphone voltage|The acoustic pain threshold|Zero dBFS is the reference full-scale digital level.|DIG
studio|Why can a lookahead audio processor increase monitoring delay?|It buffers audio before processing|It increases the song tempo|It changes the instrument tuning|It converts mono to stereo|Lookahead processing must delay audio to inspect upcoming peaks.|ALAT
